  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public health law, in relation to newborn screening for\nduchenne muscular dystrophy",
    "basePrintNo": "S6814",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6814-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §2500-a, Pub Health L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S6814\n\nSPONSOR: RIVERA\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public health law, in relation to newborn screening\nfor duchenne muscular dystrophy\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis bill adds Duchenne muscular dystrophy to the list of diseases and\nconditions that newborns are required to be tested for.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill authorizes the Commissioner of Health to add Duch-\nenne muscular dystrophy to the list of diseases and conditions that\nevery infant under 28 days of age must be tested for once the Wadsworth\nCenter validates such test.\n\nSection 2 of the bill includes the effective date\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nDuchenne muscular dystrophy (Duchenne), the most common form of muscular\ndystrophy, is a fatal genetic pediatric disease that results in progres-\nsive muscle deterioration and weakness, which causes an inability to\nwalk and feed oneself and to breathe unassisted over time. Duchenne is\ncaused by a gene mutation and affects approximately one out of every\n3,500 to 5,000 male births with most patient's dying in their mid-to-\nlate twenties.\n\nThe average age for diagnosis of Duchenne is five years of age, which is\non average 2.5 years after parents or caregivers typically first notice\nsymptoms of the disease. This diagnosis delay is often worse for fami-\nlies of color and families of lower socioeconomic status.\n\nEarly detection of Duchenne is important as it is a non-reversible\nprogressive disease for which there are currently multiple treatments\nthat significantly slow the progression of the disease, four of which\nare approved by the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) for use from\nthe date of birth. Additionally, there are several gene therapies\ntargeting the mutated gene that causes Duchenne that are currently in\ntrials.\n\nNewborn screening for detecting Duchenne is currently available as the\nFDA has already approved a lab test that uses the same blood spots that\nare collected at birth. New York currently requires newborn screening\nfor over 50 diseases and conditions including congenital heart defects,\nadrenoleukodystrophy, hypothyroidism, HIV, and Cystic Fibrosis.\n\nAdding Duchenne to the list of conditions and diseases that are screened\nfor newborns will help identify individuals who should begin receiving\nappropriate care and treatments sooner and will help eliminate the\nracial and socioeconomic disparities of diagnosis and treatment of Duch-\nenne.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNone\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the 120th day after it shall become law.\nEffective immediately, the Commissioner of Health shall make regulations\nand take other actions reasonably necessary to implement this act on\nthat date.\n",
